# 21. Cross-Screen Functional Requirements

## FR-CROSS-001 — Shared Event Identity

If the user selects an active event, all relevant screens must reference the same event identity.

---

## FR-CROSS-002 — Persistent Context

Relevant context must survive navigation.

---

## FR-CROSS-003 — Consistent Status

If an event changes state, all screens representing that event must eventually reflect the updated state.

---

## FR-CROSS-004 — Event Timeline Integrity

Significant state changes must remain visible in the event history.

---

## FR-CROSS-005 — Evidence Integrity

Evidence must retain its relationship to the event and the conclusion it supports.

---

## FR-CROSS-006 — Uncertainty Integrity

Uncertainty must remain visible when moving between screens.

---

## FR-CROSS-007 — No Silent Mutation

A read-only interaction must not unexpectedly modify operational state.

---

## FR-CROSS-008 — No Screen-Owned Reality

No screen may create an isolated version of the event that contradicts the global state.

---

# 22. Global Loading Requirements

Every screen that retrieves dynamic data must support:

```text
Initial Loading
Partial Loading
Refreshing
Background Update
Connection Loss
Recovery After Connection Loss
```

Loading states must preserve enough surrounding context that users understand what is being loaded.

---

# 23. Global Empty-State Requirements

Empty states must distinguish between:

```text
No Data Exists
No Matching Data
Data Not Yet Available
Data Unavailable
No Active Event
No Relevant Evidence
```

These must never all appear as the generic:

> "No data."

---

# 24. Global Error Requirements

Errors must communicate:

1. what failed
2. what information may be affected
3. whether the current state can still be trusted
4. what the user can do next
5. whether retry is possible

For high-consequence actions, failure must never appear as successful execution.

---

# 25. Global Real-Time Requirements

AEGIS is intended to support changing operational conditions.

Where real-time behavior is implemented:

```text
New Signal
   ↓
State Update
   ↓
Affected Domain
   ↓
Affected Screens
   ↓
User Notification / Visual Update
```

The architecture must avoid uncontrolled full-application refreshes when a localized state update is sufficient.

---

# 26. Global Audit Requirements

Auditable events include, where applicable:

```text
Event Creation
Threat Classification
Risk Assessment
Investigation Update
Case Relationship Update
Transmission Finding
Containment Proposal
Containment Authorization
Containment Execution
Response Action
Response Status Change
Recovery Status Change
Preparedness Lesson
Permission-Sensitive Action
```

The final audit architecture determines exact schemas.

---

# 27. Permission Architecture Requirement

Each screen must distinguish:

```text
Can View
Can Investigate
Can Assess
Can Recommend
Can Authorize
Can Execute
Can Modify
Can Close
```

These permissions must never be inferred solely from the existence of a UI control.

The backend must enforce authorization independently.

---

# 28. Accessibility Requirements

AEGIS must support accessible operation across the product.

At minimum:

- keyboard navigation
- readable typography
- sufficient contrast
- non-color-only status communication
- semantic structure
- meaningful labels
- accessible interactive controls
- understandable alerts
- predictable navigation

High-density telemetry must not become an excuse for inaccessible information presentation.

